#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079624 :tutorial/content "When simplifying expressions, we prefer not to leave a radical in the denominator. To eliminate a radical in an expression's denominator, we can use a process known as **rationalizing the denominator**.\n\nTo rationalize the denominator, we need to multiply both the numerator and denominator by the radical that we wish to remove from the denominator.\n\nFor instance, to rationalize the denominator in the expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nall we need to do is multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{x}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{x}} &=  \\\\\n(\\sqrt{x})/((\\sqrt{x})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the final expression no longer has a radical in the denominator. Thus, the denominator has been rationalized.\n\n**Note:** Because we multiplied the numerator and denominator by the same amount, the resulting expression\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x}}{x}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to the original fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nThey are equivalent, even though they are written differently." :tutorial/id #uuid "17e8e5bd-0b03-5315-8d7a-fc97948fb0d4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1614 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
